{"name":"Wafer","hex":"#d4bbb1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wafer","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light, thin, flat biscuit/cookie.","A thin disk of consecrated unleavened bread used in communion."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To seal or fasten with a wafer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wafer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wafer","blocks":[{"text":"A wafer is a crisp, often sweet, very thin, flat, light biscuit, often used to decorate ice cream, and also used as a garnish on some sweet dishes. They frequently have a waffle surface pattern but may also be patterned with insignia of the food's manufacturer or may be patternless. Some chocolate bars, such as Kit Kat and Toffee Crisp, are wafers with chocolate in and around them."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Akbar_Mashti.JPG/330px-Akbar_Mashti.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wafer","wikidata":"Q117072235"},{"title":"Wafer (electronics)","blocks":[{"text":"In electronics, a wafer is a thin slice of semiconductor, such as a crystalline silicon, used for the fabrication of integrated circuits and, in photovoltaics, to manufacture solar cells."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/ICC_2008_Poland_Silicon_Wafer_1_edit.png/330px-ICC_2008_Poland_Silicon_Wafer_1_edit.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wafer_(electronics)","wikidata":"Q267131"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Karlsbader-Oblate.jpg/500px-Karlsbader-Oblate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Karlsbader-Oblate.jpg/1280px-Karlsbader-Oblate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wik